          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Lucretius</strong><br>\nNot much is known about the early life of Titus Lucretius Carus, but he was a Roman poet and philosopher who lived during the 1st century BCE. His work reflects the Epicurean philosophy, which values the pursuit of knowledge and the joy of living without fear of the gods or death. Lucretius was likely a member of the Roman elite, educated in science, philosophy, and rhetoric—skills that shine through in his poetry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>His Masterwork \"De Rerum Natura\"</strong><br>\nLucretius is best known for his only surviving work, \"De Rerum Natura\" or \"On the Nature of Things,\" a long poetic treatise on philosophy. Written in six books, this epic poem explores Epicurean physics and ethics, aiming to free its readers from the fear of death and the divine. It provides a detailed exposition of the atomic theory of Democritus as understood by Epicurus.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Epicureanism Explained</strong><br>\nEpicureanism, the heart of Lucretius's philosophy, taught that the world is composed of atoms moving in empty space. It suggested that gods exist but are not concerned with human affairs. The goal of life, according to this view, is to achieve tranquility and freedom from fear, as well as absence of pain, through understanding the workings of the world and the limits of desires.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
