          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Lucretius's View on Religion</strong><br>\nLucretius was critical of religion and superstition, which he believed often led to human misery and conflict. He argued that religious fears are born from a lack of understanding of the natural world. By demystifying natural phenomena, he aimed to show that the world could be explained by natural processes without resorting to the intervention of the gods.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contribution to Science and Philosophy</strong><br>\nThrough \"De Rerum Natura,\" Lucretius made significant contributions to the Roman understanding of science and philosophy. He bridged the gap between Greek and Roman thought, translating complex Greek philosophical ideas into the Roman cultural context. His work preserves the atomistic theory, which would eventually contribute to the development of modern science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Poetry and Literary Style</strong><br>\nAs a poet, Lucretius's style is characterized by its clarity, philosophical depth, and rhythmic beauty. He uses vivid imagery and metaphors to elucidate abstract philosophical ideas, making them accessible and engaging. His literary contributions have influenced poets and thinkers throughout history, beyond the scope of his philosophical impact.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
