          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Influence of \"De Rerum Natura\"</strong><br>\nThe rediscovery of \"De Rerum Natura\" during the Renaissance greatly influenced modern thought and science. Its detailed descriptions of atomism were revolutionary for that time. The poem's arguments against the fear of death and the supernatural had a liberating effect on the intellectual climate of the period.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reception and Legacy</strong><br>\nIn ancient times, Lucretius's work received mixed reactions due to its controversial content. However, over time, \"De Rerum Natura\" has been celebrated for its insights into nature and its elegiac quality. Lucretius's influence can be seen in the works of later philosophers and writers, including the Enlightenment thinkers who valued his rational approach to understanding the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modern Interpretations and Relevance</strong><br>\nToday, scholars continue to study Lucretius's text for its rich interweaving of science and poetry. His attempts to grapple with existential questions through rationalism have made \"De Rerum Natura\" a timeless piece. In a modern context, Lucretius's works remind us of the importance of scientific inquiry and the value of viewing the world through a lens free from superstition.</p>\n"
              },
